.TH COFF_GET_SYMTAB 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
coff_get_symtab \- Retrieve the symbol table from a COFF file

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/coff.h>

.IB coff_symtab_entry*
.IB coff_get_symtab (void* coff, uint32* num);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBcoff_get_symtab\fR function retrieves the symbol table from a COFF (Common Object File Format) file. It takes a COFF file pointer \fBcoff\fR and a pointer to a \fBuint32\fR variable \fBnum\fR as parameters. The function returns a pointer to the symbol table entry, and optionally sets the number of symbols in the symbol table.

.SH coff_get_symtab
The \fBcoff_get_symtab\fR function retrieves the symbol table from a COFF file. It takes a COFF file pointer \fBcoff\fR and a pointer to a \fBuint32\fR variable \fBnum\fR as parameters. The function returns a pointer to the symbol table entry, and optionally sets the number of symbols in the symbol table.

The function returns a pointer to the symbol table entry, or \fBNULL\fR if the symbol table is not found or an error occurs.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBcoff_get_symtab\fR function to retrieve the symbol table from a COFF file:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/coff.h>

int main() {
    void* coff_file = load_coff_file("example.coff");
    uint32 num_symtab_entries = 0;
    coff_symtab_entry* symtab = coff_get_symtab(coff_file, &num_symtab_entries);

    if(symtab != NULL) {
        // Use the symbol table

        printf("Number of symbols: %u\n", num_symtab_entries);
    } else {
        printf("Failed to retrieve symbol table\n");
    }

    free_coff_file(coff_file);

    return 0;
}
.nef